    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The battle brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, whose rule had reached north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> in 1758, into direct conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>, which the Marathas' hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> had threatened.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> were besieged for about two months in their lines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> led them out on 13 January because supplies had run short.</li><li>The killing did not end with the fighting: the best eyewitness chronicle reports about 40,000 prisoners killed the day after, and about 22,000 women and children were taken as slaves.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The battle perhaps had the largest number of fatalities in a single day reported in a classic formation battle between two armies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> brought roughly 200,000 non-combatants, among them pilgrims, with their army.</p></aside>
    </section>

