    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1758</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> with Adina Beg Khan and drive out Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>, extending their rule north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1759</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> raises an army and wins over Najib-ud-Daula, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1760</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> starts north from Patdur</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1760</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> at Baghpat and skirmish at Samalkha, which leaves the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> partly encircled</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1761</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> leave their camp before dawn on 13 January and face the Afghan lines at about eight in the morning</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1761</span><span class="rf-text">The main battle is fought on 14 January; Vishwasrao is killed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> lines break</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1761</span><span class="rf-text">Prisoners are killed the day after the battle and women and children are driven off as slaves</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1761</span><span class="rf-text">Ahmad Shah leaves for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> harassing his march through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="third-battle-of-panipat" data-v="6c2349a698" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two lines face each other</b> The Maratha lines stood a little north of Kala Amb with Bhau and the royal guard in the centre, the Gardis on the left and Holkar and Sindhia on the right. The Afghan line stood to the south with Najib on its left and the Rohillas on its right centre.</li><li><b>The Gardis advance and the Rohillas fall back</b> The Maratha left, under Ibrahim Khan, advanced its infantry against the Rohillas. Point-blank salvos sent the Rohillas reeling back to their lines, and the Gardi musketeers held the field for three hours.</li><li><b>Bhau charges the Afghan left centre</b> Bhau led a charge against the forces under Shah Wali. The attack nearly broke the Afghan lines, but Shuja-ud-Daulah did not move from his position and the Maratha mounts were exhausted.</li><li><b>Scindia attacks Najib and the Afghan reserve is called up</b> The Marathas under Scindia attacked Najib, who fought a defensive action and kept them at bay. Meanwhile Ahmad Shah called up his 15,000 reserve troops from his camp.</li><li><b>The Afghan reserves charge</b> Fresh reserves went to support Shah Wali against Bhau in the centre. Some 7,000 Maratha cavalry and infantry were killed before the hand-to-hand fighting began.</li><li><b>The Gardis are encircled</b> The Maratha cavalry guarding the Gardis broke its position and went out against the Rohillas. The Rohilla riflemen fired on it, then encircled the Gardis and outflanked the Maratha centre while Shah Wali pressed the front.</li><li><b>Holkar and Scindia leave the field</b> Realising the battle was lost, Holkar's and Scindia's contingents merged, and one broke from the Maratha right and escaped south through an opening in the Durrani lines. Bhau and the royal forces fought on.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau.jpg" alt="Sadashivrao Bhau" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_portrait_in_an_oval_of_the_Bhao,_Sadhashir_Rao,.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deccani School, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1757 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> made his son Timur Shah governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. Jahan Khan, who ruled for the young governor, was harsh, and Adina Beg Khan, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bist-doab">Jalandhar Doab</a>, turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, who had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> after the Battle of Delhi (1757). With his help the Marathas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and in 1758 Adina Beg was made subahdar of the Punjab in return for a yearly tribute of seventy five lakh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>.</p><p>This was the high-water mark of Maratha expansion, with their rule running from north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> spoke of placing his son Vishwasrao on the Mughal throne. Shah Waliullah and other Muslim clergy were alarmed and appealed to Ahmad Shah Abdali. In 1759 he raised an army whose core was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, with Pashtun tribes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a>, other Muslims of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, who by late July 1760 chose what was seen as the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>The Maratha command went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> after Raghunathrao asked for more troops than Bhau, his cousin, would give and declined to go north. Bhau gathered between 45,000 and 60,000 troops, with roughly 200,000 non-combatants, some of them pilgrims bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> holy sites.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali.jpg" alt="Ahmad Shah Abdali" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Abdali</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ahmad-Shah_Durrani._Mughal_miniature._ca._1757,_Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle set the artillery, musketry and cavalry of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, jezail musketry and mounted zamburak artillery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> and Najib-ud-Daula. Over 125,000 troops were involved. The Marathas had almost 150 French-made guns, with a range of over a kilometre, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Bhau</a> planned to break the Afghans with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire and to hold his cavalry back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> had numerical and qualitative superiority, and the Maratha artillery was static and lacked mobility.</p><p>Before the battle the Jat ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a> advised leaving the families and baggage behind and fighting with light cavalry. Bhau found this dishonourable, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> later withdrew their support. The Marathas, besieged for two months, were cut off by Afghan forces on every side. Bhau led out his army on 13 January, with Ibrahim Khan Gardi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> on the left, Holkar and Sindhia on the right and himself with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s son in the centre. The line was about 12 km across, with the civilians behind it.</p><p>The Afghan line lay a few metres south of today's Sanauli Road. Najib held the left, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, with 3,000 soldiers and 50 to 60 cannon, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shah Wali, with 19,000 mailed horsemen, held the left centre, and 15,000 Rohillas under Hafiz Rahmat the right centre. Ahmad Shah stood in the rear of the line and had 60 smooth-bore cannon.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula.jpg" alt="Shuja-ud-Daula" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja-ud-daula,_Nawab_of_Oudh.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tilly Kettle, 1735-1786, British, active in India (from 1769 to 1776), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before dawn on 14 January the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> broke their fast on sugared water and moved their guns into place about 2 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. Ibrahim Khan's infantry led the attack, the first Afghan charge by Najib's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> was broken, and for three hours the 8,000 Gardi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> killed about 12,000 Rohillas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Bhau</a> then led a charge at the Afghan left centre under Shah Wali, which nearly broke the line, but the Marathas' half-starved horses were exhausted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> held his place, and Ahmad Shah sent musketeers to shoot deserters back into the field.</p><p>By noon it looked as though Bhau would win. Ahmad Shah called up 15,000 reserve troops and 2,000 swivel-mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> guns, which fired over the heads of his own infantry at the Maratha cavalry. About 7,000 Maratha cavalry and infantry were killed before hand-to-hand fighting began at about 14:00, and by 16:00 the tired infantry gave way to fresh Afghan reserves.</p><p>Bhau came down from his elephant to lead the fight, and Vishwasrao had already been killed by a shot to the head. Afghan prisoners taken at Kunjpura are said to have risen within the Maratha camp, and Vitthal Vinchurkar and Damaji Gaikwad broke position, leaving the Gardis to be encircled. Some Maratha artillery fought until sunset, and many troops escaped that night.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Third Battle of Panipat&#8221; cites where it covers Afghan defeat at Kunjpura, Afghans cross Yamuna and the Battles of Samalkha and Meerut, Formations, Massacres after the battle:</p><ul><li>Casi Raja Pundit, An Account Of The Last Battle of Panipat (Oxford University Press, 1926)</li><li>Shejwalkar, Trimbak S., Panipat 1761 (Deccan College, 1946)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia.jpg" alt="Jassa Singh Ahluwalia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equestrian_painting_of_Jassa_Singh_Ahluwalia_on_horseback_in-front_of_his_haveli_with_a_fly-whisk_attendant,_circa_late_18th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between 60,000 and 70,000 troops are believed to have been killed in the fighting; the extent of the losses on both sides is heavily disputed, and the numbers of injured and prisoners vary considerably. After the defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> troops fell on the fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and killed soldiers and civilians. Afghan officers who had lost kin were allowed to carry out massacres the next day, in Panipat and around it, and heaped mounds of severed heads outside their camps.</p><p>According to the bakhar of Kashi Raja, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, about 40,000 Maratha prisoners were killed the day after the battle. Hamilton, a reporter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Gazette, also gives 40,000 executed. Qutb Shah's son killed 4,000 fugitives near Sonepat and Abdus Samad Khan's son 5,000 near Bahadurgad, and some 22,000 women and children were driven off as slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> are said to have saved 2,000 of the prisoners. The Siyar-ut-Mutakhirin describes prisoners paraded, given parched grain and water, and beheaded.</p><p>Jankoji Scindia was taken and executed at Najib's instigation, and Ibrahim Khan Gardi was tortured and executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Bhau</a>'s wife Parvatibai escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, and some 15,000 soldiers reached Gwalior. Ahmad Shah named Najib-ud-Daula regent to the Mughal emperor and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in March 1761.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The single best eyewitness chronicle is the bakhar by Kashi Raja, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, who was an ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. The British historian Grant Duff's History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> includes an interview with a survivor of the massacres and generally corroborates the figure of 40,000, and the Siyar-ut-Mutakhirin also describes them. Shejwalkar's monograph Panipat 1761 is often regarded as the best secondary source, and his own total is not less than 100,000 Marathas, soldiers and non-combatants, killed during and after the battle.</p><p>The figures are where the accounts part. The losses on both sides are heavily disputed and the numbers of injured and prisoners vary considerably. The 60,000 to 70,000 killed in the fighting is what historians are said to believe, not a count. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Gazette's Hamilton puts about half a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> people in Panipat town, while the figure given for the Maratha non-combatants on the march is roughly 200,000.</p><p>Historians also differ on why the Marathas lost. Accounts of the defeat list Afghan numbers and mobility, the quarrels among Maratha chiefs, the distance of about 1,000 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Bhau</a>'s lack of knowledge of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Some hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, as Holkar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a> advised, could not have been carried out with the camp followers, and that if Holkar had stayed the defeat would have been delayed but not averted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia.jpg" alt="Mahadji Scindia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Scindia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahadaji_Sindhia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Wales, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> never fully recovered from Panipat but remained the largest empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> ten years later. The result was a temporary halt to their advance in the north and about ten years of destabilisation. Madhavrao is credited with reviving their power, and in 1771 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Shinde</a> led an army into the north to restore Maratha supremacy. After Madhavrao's early death, at 28, the chiefs fell into infighting, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> annexed their lands in 1819.</p><p>For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> victory ended Maratha plans to take it and left the Durranis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> as the two main powers. The Sikhs harassed Ahmad Shah's march home, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a>, who stayed out of the battle, are said to have helped the Marathas who escaped. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> were rewarded with grants, and the Mughal empire, still nominal, officially ended in 1857.</p><p>Ahmad Shah praised the Marathas' valour in a letter to Madho Singh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, and the battle is also remembered as a scene of valour on both sides. It has been the subject of a poem by Kipling, a Bengali poem by Kaykobad, a play by Munier Choudhury, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> novel by Vishwas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patil">Patil</a> and a 2019 film that has several major historical inaccuracies.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Third Battle of Panipat, fought on 14 January 1761, pitted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> and its Indian allies. The Afghan side won. An eyewitness chronicle reports about 40,000 Maratha prisoners killed the next day, and some 22,000 women and children were driven off as slaves.</em></p>

